        How to Paste an Image to Search on Google, Bing, and TinEye
    </h1>

    <AuthorByline publishDate="2026-09-29" />

    <figure class="my-6 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas-soft">
        <img src="/blog/paste-image-to-search.webp" alt="Screenshot of PasteDownloadApp with a pasted 1200 by 900 lighthouse illustration, ready to save as a file before running a reverse image search" width="1600" height="900" class="w-full aspect-[16/9] object-cover" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
    </figure>

    <div class="text-body text-base sm:text-[17px] leading-relaxed space-y-4 [&_h2]:text-ink [&_h2]:text-lg [&_h2]:sm:text-xl [&_h2]:font-semibold [&_h2]:tracking-tight [&_h2]:mt-8 [&_h2]:mb-2 [&_h2]:leading-tight [&_h3]:text-ink [&_h3]:text-base [&_h3]:sm:text-lg [&_h3]:font-semibold [&_h3]:tracking-tight [&_h3]:mt-6 [&_h3]:mb-2 [&_h3]:leading-tight [&_a]:text-link [&_a]:underline [&_a]:underline-offset-2 [&_a]:decoration-link/30 [&_a]:transition-colors [&_a:hover]:decoration-link">

        <p>You see a chair you like in a screenshot, a photo that looks suspiciously too good for the story it is attached to, or a plant you cannot name. The quickest way to find out more is a reverse image search: you give a search engine the picture instead of words. Most people assume that means saving the image as a file and uploading it. It does not have to. Google, Bing, and TinEye all accept a pasted image, straight from your clipboard.</p>

        <p>This guide covers exactly where to paste on each of the three, the browser shortcuts that skip copying entirely, how to do it on a phone, and which engine is best for which kind of question. It also covers what to do when the paste is ignored, which usually comes down to what is actually on your clipboard.</p>

        <h2>Before You Paste: Get the Image, Not the Link</h2>

        <p>Paste-to-search only works if the clipboard holds image data. That sounds obvious, but it is the most common reason it fails, because several everyday actions put something else on the clipboard:</p>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li><strong>"Copy image address"</strong> copies the URL as text. Some search boxes accept a URL too, but a paste into an image drop area expects a picture.</li>
            <li><strong>Copying a file</strong> in File Explorer or Finder copies a reference to the file, not the pixels inside it. Most web pages cannot read that as an image. Drag the file in instead.</li>
            <li><strong>A screenshot saved to disk</strong> is not on the clipboard at all unless you used a copy-to-clipboard shortcut.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The ways that do put a real image on the clipboard: right-click a picture in your browser and choose <strong>Copy image</strong>; on Windows, press <strong>Win+Shift+S</strong> and drag a region; on a Mac, press <strong>Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4</strong> and drag, which copies the capture instead of saving it. <a href="/blog/where-do-clipboard-images-go/">Where screenshots and copied images go</a> lists the clipboard shortcuts for every platform.</p>

        <h2>Paste an Image Into Google Search (Google Lens)</h2>

        <p>Google's image search runs on Google Lens. On a computer:</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li>Go to <strong>images.google.com</strong>, or the main Google homepage.</li>
            <li>Click the camera icon in the search box. Its tooltip reads <strong>Search by image</strong>.</li>
            <li>A panel opens that says you can drag an image there or upload a file. With that panel open, press <strong>Ctrl+V</strong> or <strong>Cmd+V</strong>. The image uploads and Lens shows results.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>The same panel also has a box where you can paste an image link instead, which is handy when you copied the address rather than the image. Once results appear, Lens lets you drag a selection box over part of the image. That is worth doing: a search for "the lamp in the corner" works far better when the box is around the lamp rather than the whole room.</p>

        <h3>Skip the Clipboard in Chrome</h3>

        <p>If the image is already on a web page in Chrome, you do not need to copy it at all. Right-click the image and choose <strong>Search image with Google</strong> (older versions called this Search image with Google Lens). Results open in a side panel. Chrome can also search anything visible on the screen, not just images: look for <strong>Search with Google Lens</strong> in the right-click menu on the page or in Chrome's three-dot menu, then drag a box around what you want to search. That works on video frames, parts of a PDF open in the browser, and images that block right-clicking.</p>

        <VideoLink
