        <h2>Paste an Image Into Bing Visual Search</h2>

        <p>Bing's version is called Visual Search. Go to <strong>bing.com/images</strong> and click the camera icon in the search box. A panel opens where you can drag an image, browse for a file, or paste an image or URL. Press <strong>Ctrl+V</strong> or <strong>Cmd+V</strong> with the panel open and Bing runs the search. Like Lens, it lets you adjust a crop box on the results page to focus on one object.</p>

        <p>In Microsoft Edge there is also a built-in shortcut: right-click an image on any page and choose the visual search option, which opens Bing results in the sidebar. The exact label varies between Edge versions.</p>

        <h2>Paste an Image Into TinEye</h2>

        <p>TinEye is a dedicated reverse image search engine, and it supports paste directly. Copy the image, go to <strong>tineye.com</strong>, and press <strong>Ctrl+V</strong> or <strong>Cmd+V</strong> anywhere on the search page. <a href="https://blog.tineye.com/search-tineye-with-copy-and-paste/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TinEye's own blog</a> describes this. You can also drag a file onto the page or paste an image URL into the search box.</p>

        <p>TinEye works differently from Lens and Visual Search. It looks for copies of the same image, including resized, cropped, and slightly edited versions, rather than visually similar things. Results can be sorted by the date TinEye first found each copy, which is exactly what you want when you are trying to find where an image first appeared.</p>

