        <p>For anything that matters, such as checking whether a photo in a news story is old or taken somewhere else, try more than one. Each engine has its own index, and an image missing from one is often found by another.</p>

        <h2>Searching From a Phone</h2>

        <p>On Android, the Google app and Chrome both have Lens built in. In Chrome, long-press an image and choose the Google Lens search option. In the Google app, tap the camera icon in the search bar and pick a photo or screenshot from your gallery. Many Android phones also offer Circle to Search: hold the home button or navigation bar, then circle whatever is on the screen.</p>

        <p>On iPhone, the Google app has the same Lens camera icon in its search bar, and Chrome for iOS offers an image search option when you long-press an image. In Safari, long-pressing an image offers Look Up on photos where Apple's Visual Look Up recognizes a subject. It is Apple's own recognition, not a web search, but it is a quick first check for plants, pets, and landmarks.</p>

        <p>Pasting into a search engine's website from a phone is less reliable than on a computer, because mobile browsers handle clipboard images differently. <a href="/blog/mobile-clipboard-android-vs-ios/">Saving a clipboard image on Android vs iPhone</a> explains the differences. If paste will not cooperate, upload the saved screenshot instead.</p>

        <h2>When Pasting Does Not Work</h2>

        <p>If you press Ctrl+V and nothing happens, work through these in order:</p>

        <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-1.5">
            <li><strong>Open the upload panel first.</strong> On Google and Bing, the paste only registers while the camera panel is open. Pasting into the main text box searches for text instead.</li>
            <li><strong>Check what is on the clipboard.</strong> Paste into a chat app or document. If you get a link or a file name, copy the image itself again.</li>
            <li><strong>Click inside the page</strong> before pasting, so the tab has keyboard focus.</li>
            <li><strong>Save it as a file and upload it.</strong> Every engine accepts an uploaded file, whatever browser you use. <a href="https://pastedownloadapp.com/">PasteDownloadApp</a> turns the clipboard image into a PNG, JPEG, or WebP file in one paste, and the conversion happens in your browser, so nothing is uploaded until you choose to upload it to the search engine.</li>
        </ol>

        <p>Saving a file first is also the better route when you want to search the same image on all three engines. You upload one file three times instead of copying it again for each site.</p>

        <h2>Crop First for Better Results</h2>

        <p>Reverse image search works best when the image contains one clear subject. A full-screen screenshot with a browser toolbar, a taskbar, and three product photos gives the engine too much to match. Cropping to just the thing you care about almost always improves results. Lens and Visual Search let you adjust the crop after searching, but TinEye matches the whole image, so a tight crop matters more there. The simplest method is to take the screenshot of just that region in the first place, with Win+Shift+S or Cmd+Ctrl+Shift+4.</p>

        <h2>A Note on Privacy</h2>

        <p>Every search engine here uploads your image to its servers to run the search. That is how the search works, and it is worth remembering when the screenshot includes more than you meant to share: an email address in the corner, a name on a message, a document in the background. Crop those out before you paste. Google and Bing may keep searched images according to their privacy policies, and TinEye's policy says it does not keep images you search with, but check each service's current policy if it matters to you.</p>

        <p>PasteDownloadApp itself never uploads anything. It is a safe place to paste, check, and save an image before you decide which search engine to send it to. The <a href="/blog/save-clipboard-image-without-software/">guide to saving clipboard images without software</a> explains how that works.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <h3>Can I paste a screenshot directly into Google to search it?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Open Google Images, click the camera icon, and press Ctrl+V or Cmd+V while the upload panel is open. The screenshot must be on your clipboard as an image, which means taking it with a copy-to-clipboard shortcut or copying it from wherever it is displayed.</p>

        <h3>Why does Google search for text when I paste an image?</h3>
        <p>You pasted into the main search box rather than the image panel, or your clipboard held text such as an image URL. Click the camera icon first, then paste.</p>

        <h3>Is reverse image search free?</h3>
        <p>Google Lens, Bing Visual Search, and TinEye's standard search are all free to use. TinEye sells paid plans for high-volume and commercial use.</p>

        <h3>Can I search with an image on a website that blocks right-clicking?</h3>
        <p>Yes. Take a screenshot of the area with a copy-to-clipboard shortcut and paste that, or use Chrome's Search with Google Lens option to select that part of the screen.</p>

